# grep can search across multiple files and folders

# To search inside folders (directories), you must use the -r (recursive) or -R flags. This tells grep to dive into the folder, look through every file inside it, and move down into any subfolders automatically.

# To search an entire folder and all of its subdirectories, use the -r flag.

# Syntax
grep -r "pattern" /path/to/folder

# Example: Search for the word "database" in all files inside /var/log/
grep -r "database" /var/log/

# Output format: grep will print the paths to the files along with the exact lines where it found the match 

(e.g., /var/log/app.log: connection to database failed).


# Searching Multiple Specific Files
# If you don't want to search a whole folder, you can list individual files separated by spaces, or use wildcards (*).

# Example A: Search in three specific files
grep "error" file1.log file2.log file3.log

# Example B: Search in all files ending with .conf in the current folder
grep "Port" *.conf

# Combining Flags for targeted Folder Searches
# Grouping Folder Searches with -l (List Files Only)
# combining -r with -l yields a clean list of filenames across your whole directory structure.

# Find the names of all files in a project folder that contain the word "TODO"
grep -rl "TODO" /home/user/project/

# If you are searching a massive directory but only want to look inside specific file formats, use --include or --exclude.

# Search for "API_KEY" only inside Python (.py) files inside a directory
grep -r --include="*.py" "API_KEY" /home/user/code/

# Search a folder but skip all heavy log files
grep -r --exclude="*.log" "critical_error" /var/www/



