# In Linux scripting and terminal commands, conditional logic (AND, OR, NOT) is used to link multiple commands, validate variables, or filter text data.

# Because Linux relies on both exit status codes (where 0 means Success/True and anything else means Failure/False) and text processing, these logic concepts show up in two main areas: Command Chaining (the shell level) and grep Text Filtering (the data level).


1. The Shell Level: Chaining Commands

# You can control script flow by connecting multiple terminal commands based on whether the previous ones succeeded or failed.


The AND Operator (&&)

# The second command executes only if the first command succeeds (returns an exit status of 0).
# • Analogy: "If step A goes well, move to step B."
mkdir /var/log/my_app && cd /var/log/my_app


The OR Operator (||)

# The second command executes only if the first command fails (returns a non-zero exit status).
# Analogy: "Try step A. If it breaks, do step B as a backup."
grep -q "error" system.log || echo "System is clean!"

The NOT Operator (!)
# Reverses the outcome of a condition or command. It turns a success into a failure and vice versa.
# Run the code inside ONLY if the file does NOT exist
if [ ! -f /etc/config.json ]; then
    echo "Configuration file missing!"
fi


2. The Data Level: grep Text Filtering

# When parsing logs or configurations, you often need to match complex string logic. To achieve this, you use Extended Regular Expressions (grep -E)

grep AND Logic

#  standard grep processes text line-by-line, it doesn’t have a built-in "AND" operator keyword. However, you can easily perform an AND operation—finding lines that contain both Pattern A and Pattern B

# The first grep filters out lines containing the first keyword, and the second grep searches only within those filtered lines for the second keyword.

# Syntax
grep "PatternA" filename | grep "PatternB"

# Practical Example: Find logs where "User1" successfully "Logged-in"
grep "User1" /var/log/auth.log | grep "Logged-in"

# • Why it’s great: You can chain as many as you want (grep A | grep B | grep C).


grep OR Logic

# To find lines that contain either one word or another, use grep -E (Extended Regex) combined with the pipe | character.

# Example: Find lines containing "CRITICAL" or "FATAL":
grep -E "CRITICAL|FATAL" /var/log/syslog


grep NOT Logic

# To exclude specific terms, use the -v (invert match) flag we discussed earlier.
grep -v "DEBUG" /var/log/nginx/access.log
